{"description":"Write the three documents the MVP cannot ship without.\n\nA quickstart for a non-technical reader, reaching a working session in three commands. `walkie help` covering every command from inside the tool. And an operations runbook for the coordinator.\n\nTwo things must be stated explicitly rather than left to discovery, because both are consequences of recorded decisions. First, remote shell access is Tailscale SSH's job — walkie/adr:005-no-remote-command-execution declines it deliberately, and without this pointer the capability simply reads as missing. Second, the security limitations walkie/adr:004-security-model enumerates: local history is unencrypted, there is no key rotation or multi-device identity, and walkie inherits the tailnet ACL and cannot compensate for a permissive one.","acceptanceCriteria":"1. A team member who has not seen walkie before reaches a working session using the quickstart alone, without asking for help. This is verified with a real person, not assumed.\n2. `walkie help` documents every command and every build-variant difference.\n3. The quickstart points to Tailscale SSH for remote shell access and explains why walkie does not provide it.\n4. The runbook covers deployment, the tailnet ACL expectations walkie depends on, retention configuration, metric interpretation including the direct-versus-relay ratio, and recovery from a coordinator restart.\n5. The security limitations are stated in user-facing documentation, not only in the decision records.\n6. Fingerprint verification is documented as a procedure a non-technical user can actually follow."}